# Build context trim for `docker build -f docker/Dockerfile .` (root context, whole reactor).
#
# docker/Dockerfile deliberately does `COPY . .` rather than listing modules: the reactor builds
# `service`'s dependency on `qits-ci-domain` from source (it is never published), and a module list
# would have to be edited every time a module is added. This file is what makes that cheap — it
# drops everything the build does not need, so adding a module stays free and no stale output leaks
# in.

.git
.gitignore

# Maven / Quarkus build output — regenerated inside the build stage, and it is the bulk of a working
# tree here (two modules' target/, one of them holding a native binary and its build artifacts).
# Copying it in would also risk a stale target/ shadowing what the stage compiles.
**/target
**/target-ide

# The Angular client's own build output, for the same two reasons one level down. `npm install` runs
# inside the build stage, and a host node_modules is the worse half of this: it is the bulk of a
# working tree once the submodule is initialised, and it carries binaries compiled for the HOST
# rather than for the builder image — Quinoa reuses an existing node_modules rather than reinstalling
# over it, so leaking one in is not merely slow, it is how the image gets a client built by the wrong
# toolchain.
**/node_modules
service/src/main/webui/dist

# Local env / IDE cruft
.env
.idea
.vscode
.sdkmanrc

# Docs and the docker/ dir itself: nothing in the build reads them, and keeping them out means
# editing this Dockerfile or a README does not bust the `COPY . .` layer.
*.md
docs
docker
